<?xml-stylesheet type="text/xsl" href="/default.xsl"?><fr:tree xmlns:fr="http://www.forester-notes.org" xmlns:html="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace" root="false" base-url="/">
  <fr:frontmatter>
    <fr:authors></fr:authors>
    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-0012/</fr:uri>
    <fr:display-uri>def-0012</fr:display-uri>
    <fr:route>/def-0012/</fr:route>
    <fr:title text="Least upper bound property">Least upper bound property</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    A set <fr:tex display="inline">S</fr:tex> has the least upper bound property if every non-empty subset <fr:tex display="inline">T</fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline">S</fr:tex> that is bounded above has a least upper bound <fr:tex display="inline">\sup  T</fr:tex>.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
  <fr:backmatter>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors></fr:authors>
        <fr:title text="References">References</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter></fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors></fr:authors>
        <fr:title text="Context">Context</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors></fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2024</fr:year>
              <fr:month>1</fr:month>
              <fr:day>27</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/math-0004/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>math-0004</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/math-0004/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The Construction of \mathbb {R}">The Construction of <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex></fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Math Analysis</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    We start constructing <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex> by a way that it satisfies the existence theorem,
    the core of construction.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors></fr:authors>
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0003/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0003</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0003/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Existence theorem">Existence theorem</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    There exists an ordered field <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex> that satisfies the <fr:link href="/def-0012/" title="Least upper bound property" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-0012/" display-uri="def-0012" type="local">least upper bound property</fr:link>.
    Moreover <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex> contains <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex> as a subfield.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    The least-upper-bound property mentioned above is defined:
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors></fr:authors>
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-0012/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-0012</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-0012/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Least upper bound property">Least upper bound property</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A set <fr:tex display="inline">S</fr:tex> has the least upper bound property if every non-empty subset <fr:tex display="inline">T</fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline">S</fr:tex> that is bounded above has a least upper bound <fr:tex display="inline">\sup  T</fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Why do we need the least-upper-bound property?
    Consider the set <fr:tex display="inline">S = \{x \in  \mathbb {Q} | x^2 &lt; 2\}</fr:tex>.
    <fr:tex display="inline">S</fr:tex> is bounded above by <fr:tex display="inline">2</fr:tex>, but it does not have a least upper bound in <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex>.
    Therefore we can&apos;t express <fr:tex display="inline">\sqrt {2}</fr:tex> in field <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex> since some &quot;gaps&quot; exist.
    This fact motivates us to construct a more complete field <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex>.
    We have constructed <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Z}</fr:tex>, and now we construct <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Then we should find a way to express &quot;<fr:tex display="inline">\sqrt {2}</fr:tex>&quot; using <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex>.
    A crucial idea is <html:strong>approximating</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline">\sqrt {2}</fr:tex> by a sequence of rational numbers.
    <fr:tex display="block">
        \sqrt {2} := \{ p^2&lt;2 \lor  p&lt;0, p\in \mathbb {Q} \}
    </fr:tex>
    We can cut the number axis into two pieces by <fr:tex display="inline">\sqrt {2}</fr:tex>, such cut is called a <html:strong>Dedekind cut</html:strong>. 
    A cut should be well-defined rather than just an intuitive concept.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    As we use set theory to construct <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex>, it motivates us to define Dedekind cut as a set.
    It should satisfies some properties:
    <html:ul><html:li>Can&apos;t be empty or the whole <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li>Closed downward</html:li>
        <html:li>Contains not the largest number</html:li></html:ul>
    A formal definition is given below:
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors></fr:authors>
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-0013/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-0013</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-0013/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Dedekind cuts">Dedekind cuts</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A Dedekind cut is a partition of the rationals <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex> into two non-empty sets <fr:tex display="inline">L</fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline">R</fr:tex> such that:
    <html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline">L\neq \emptyset </fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li><fr:tex display="inline">R\neq \emptyset </fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li>if <fr:tex display="inline">x,y\in \mathbb {Q}, x&lt;y</fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline">y\in  L</fr:tex> then <fr:tex display="inline">x\in  L</fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li>if <fr:tex display="inline">p\in  L</fr:tex> then exists <fr:tex display="inline">q\in  L</fr:tex> such that <fr:tex display="inline">p&lt;q</fr:tex></html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Now we can defined the real number as a set of Dedekind cuts.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors></fr:authors>
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-0014/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-0014</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-0014/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Real Number System">Real Number System</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The element of <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex> is a <fr:link href="/def-0013/" title="Dedekind cuts" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-0013/" display-uri="def-0013" type="local">Dedekind Cut</fr:link> in <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {Q}</fr:tex>.
    <fr:tex display="block">
        \mathbb {R} := \{ L | (L,R) \text { is a Dedekind Cut} \}
    </fr:tex></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Now define the order relation on <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex>.
    We have defined <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex> as the set of Dedekind cuts, so we can define the strict partial order relation <fr:tex display="inline">&lt;</fr:tex> on <fr:tex display="inline">\mathbb {R}</fr:tex> by the set operation <fr:tex display="inline">\subset </fr:tex>.
    The irreflexive, asymmetric and transitive properties are trivial. 
</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
